# Copy to `.env` and fill in. `.env` is gitignored; this file is committed.
#
# Nothing here is required. The backend runs with no environment at all, which is
# deliberate - a teammate should be able to clone the repo and run `python
# server.py` without a setup step that can go wrong.

# Secret used to sign session tokens (bank/security.py).
#
# Unset: a fresh random secret is generated per process. That is the safe default
# and it means no secret is ever committed. The only consequence is that every
# issued token stops working when the server restarts, which is worth knowing
# before you wonder why a saved Postman token started returning 401.
#
# Set it to keep tokens valid across restarts. Use a long random value, e.g.
#   python -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(32))"
# BANK_SECRET=

# Shared code that lets somebody register as an ADMIN (the admin register page
# in the frontend asks for it and sends it as `adminCode`).
#
# Unset: admin registration is closed and any adminCode is refused. That is the
# default on purpose - a deployment nobody configured cannot grow admins by
# accident. The seeded admins still work either way.
#
# This is the weakest thing that can honestly be called authentication: everyone
# who registers this way knows the same string and it cannot be revoked for one
# person. It is fine for a graded project with a seeded roster and would not be
# fine for anything real, where an existing admin promotes you and the audit log
# records who did it.
#
# Our team code. Change it if it ever gets out; nothing stores it but this file.
# BANK_ADMIN_CODE=Group2Rules!

# --- database (MongoDB Atlas) ----------------------------------------------
# Setup instructions, and the reasoning behind the choices, are in mongo.md.
#
# This file is read by bank/config.py, which server.py, tools/check_mongo.py and
# test_mongo.py all call. A real environment variable wins over anything set
# here, so
#     MONGODB_DB=simple_bank_scratch python server.py
# overrides the file for one run without editing it.
#
# SETTING MONGODB_URI IS THE SWITCH. With it set (and MONGODB_DB), the server
# uses MongoDB; without it, it runs in memory. There is no --mongo flag.
# `python server.py --memory` ignores these and runs in memory anyway.

# Your personal Atlas connection string. Get it from Atlas:
#   Database -> Connect -> Drivers -> Python 3.12 or later
#
# Replace <db_password> - angle brackets and all - with your database user's
# password. Use the password Atlas autogenerates: the string is a URL, so a
# password containing @ : / ? # [ ] has to be percent-encoded first, and an
# alphanumeric one sidesteps that.
#
# Each of us uses our OWN database user, not a shared one. It is revocable
# individually and the Atlas logs then say who did what.
#
# MONGODB_URI=mongodb+srv://USERNAME:PASSWORD@cluster0.XXXXX.mongodb.net/?retryWrites=true&w=majority&appName=Cluster0

# Which database inside the cluster to use. REQUIRED when MONGODB_URI is set -
# the server refuses to start without it rather than guessing which database you
# meant. One cluster, several databases:
#
#   simple_bank            the shared demo data - the frontend and the demo
#   simple_bank_<yourname> your own dev data - DEFAULT TO THIS ONE
#   simple_bank_test       the test suite, wiped constantly
#
# Default yourself to your own. Point at the shared one deliberately, so your
# reseed never deletes the data somebody is demoing from.
#
# MONGODB_DB=simple_bank_yourname

# test_mongo.py always uses `simple_bank_test`, whatever MONGODB_DB says, so it
# can never wipe the database you are working in. There is nothing to configure.
# It is opt-in:
#     bash:        MONGO_TESTS=1 python test_mongo.py
#     PowerShell:  $env:MONGO_TESTS = "1"; python test_mongo.py

# --- not decided -----------------------------------------------------------
# MySQL remains possible: the brief says MySQL "to be confirmed" while the
# syllabus teaches MongoDB. Atlas being set up does not settle the grading
# question, it just unblocks the Mongo path. Either way the connection string
# belongs here and never in a committed file.
#
# DATABASE_URL=mysql://bank_app:CHANGE_ME@localhost:3306/simple_bank
